Here's a concise way to run the same code inline on multiple named variables in C++:

### Using a Loop

You can use a loop to iterate over a list of variables and apply the same code to each one. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Apply the same code to each variable
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        var *= 2;
    }

    std::cout << "a = " << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << "b = " << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << "c = " << c << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Using a Function

Alternatively, you can define a function that takes a variable as an argument and apply the code to each variable by calling the function with each variable as an argument. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void double_value(int& var) {
    var *= 2;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Apply the same code to each variable
    double_value(a);
    double_value(b);
    double_value(c);

    std::cout << "a = " << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << "b = " << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << "c = " << c << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Using a Macro

You can also use a macro to apply the same code to each variable. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

#define DOUBLE_VAR(var) var *= 2

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Apply the same code to each variable
    DOUBLE_VAR(a);
    DOUBLE_VAR(b);
    DOUBLE_VAR(c);

    std::cout << "a = " << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << "b = " << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << "c = " << c << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Note that the macro approach is generally discouraged in favor of functions or loops, as it can lead to code duplication and make the code harder to maintain.

